<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Nobadia</h1><p class="st-meta">350 CE – 700 CE (approx.) · capital Faras</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The northernmost Nubian kingdom, heir to Meroë in Lower Nubia and the first to adopt Christianity in the 6th century.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>As the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kushite-empire">Kushite Empire</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meroe">Meroë</a> collapsed in the 4th century, the Noubades (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobades">Nobatae</a>), a people settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-nubia">Lower Nubia</a>, formed a kingdom between the First and Third Cataracts. Its early rulers, known archaeologically as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ballana">Ballana</a> culture, were buried in great tumuli at Ballana and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qustul">Qustul</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> crowns, horses and servants.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Nobadia's kings received Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subsidy">subsidies</a> to guard the frontier and fought the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/blemmyes">Blemmyes</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silko">King Silko</a>, in a Greek inscription at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalabsha">Kalabsha</a>, boasted of driving them from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> valley in the 5th century. The capital was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faras">Faras</a> (Pachoras). Around 543 a Miaphysite mission led by the priest Julian, backed by Empress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodora-wife-of-justinian-i">Theodora</a>, converted the court, and churches, Coptic and Greek literacy followed.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>By around 700 Nobadia was united with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/makuria">Makuria</a>, probably under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merkurios">Merkurios</a>, but kept its own governor, the eparch of Nobadia. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faras">Faras</a> became a great bishopric whose cathedral paintings are now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khartoum">Khartoum</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/nobadia.jpg" alt="Nobadia" width="320" height="416"><figcaption>Image: SimonP · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Christian_Nubia.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>350 CE – 700 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Faras</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Silko</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional religion, Miaphysite Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Nubian, Greek, Coptic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a href="/countries/sudan">Sudan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Nobadia, Nobatia, Ballana kingdom</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=525&amp;focus=nobadia" aria-label="Nobadia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5523 1358 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Nobadia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5843 1614L5870 1566M5843 1614L5862 1746M5843 1614L5833 1758"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5870 1566h0M5862 1746h0M5833 1758h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5843 1614h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5843 1614h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Faras. <a href="/atlas?year=525&amp;focus=nobadia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
